+.. _cli_history:
+
+Tracking changes with history
+=============================
+
+``sqlite-utils`` can record every change made to the rows of a table, so that you can see what changed, look at the table as it was earlier and put things back. See :ref:`python_api_history` for a full description of how this works.
+
+Use ``enable-history`` to start recording changes to one or more tables:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-history data.db places visits
+
+The rows already in each table are recorded as its starting point. From then on every insert, update and delete is recorded, whichever tool makes it.
+
+Use ``-x/--exclude`` to leave columns out of the history. This option can be used multiple times, and only when enabling history for a single table:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-history data.db pages --exclude hits
+
+If a table was previously tracked by the ``sqlite-history`` plugin its recorded history is imported. Add ``--no-import-legacy`` to leave that history where it is.
+
+To stop recording and delete the history that has been recorded for a table:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils disable-history data.db places
+
+.. _cli_history_show:
+
+Showing recorded changes
+------------------------
+
+The ``history`` command shows the changes that have been recorded, oldest first:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils history data.db
+
+.. code-block:: output
+
+    [{"version": 1, "table": "places", "op": "insert", "pk": 1, "at": "2026-11-13 09:30:00.123", "changeset": null, "row": {"id": 1, "name": "Oslo"}, "changed": {}},
+     {"version": 2, "table": "places", "op": "update", "pk": 1, "at": "2026-11-13 09:31:10.456", "changeset": null, "row": {"id": 1, "name": "Bergen"}, "changed": {"name": ["Oslo", "Bergen"]}}]
+
+Each change has a ``version`` number. ``row`` is the row after the change (or just before it was deleted), and ``changed`` shows the old and new value of each column an update changed.
+
+Add a table name to see the changes to that table, and one or more primary key values to see the history of a single row:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils history data.db places
+    sqlite-utils history data.db places 1
+
+Use ``--since`` and ``--until`` to limit the output to a range of versions, and ``--changeset`` to show the changes in one changeset. The command accepts the same :ref:`output format options <cli_query_csv>` as ``query`` - with ``--csv``, ``--tsv`` and the table formats the ``row`` and ``changed`` columns contain JSON.
+
+.. _cli_history_as_of:
+
+Viewing and restoring earlier versions
+--------------------------------------
+
+The ``as-of`` command outputs the rows of a table as they were at an earlier version:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils as-of data.db places 42
+
+Instead of a version number you can pass a timestamp, which stands for the newest version recorded at or before that time (in UTC):
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils as-of data.db places "2026-11-12 09:00" --csv
+
+The ``restore`` command puts a table back to how it was at a version, and outputs the number of rows it changed:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils restore data.db places 42
+
+Add primary key values to restore a single row:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils restore data.db places 42 1
+
+Restoring is recorded as new changes, so the history from before the restore is not lost.
+
+.. _cli_history_changesets:
+
+Changesets and undo
+-------------------
+
+The ``insert`` and ``upsert`` commands accept a ``--changeset`` option, which records everything the command changes in tables with history enabled as a single named changeset:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils insert data.db places places.json --pk id \
+      --changeset "March import"
+
+List the changesets in a database using ``changesets``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils changesets data.db
+
+.. code-block:: output
+
+    [{"id": 1, "message": "March import", "author": null, "created_at": "2026-11-13 09:30:00.123"}]
+
+To undo a changeset, pass its ID to ``undo``. This puts every row the changeset changed back to how it was before, records that as a new changeset and outputs the new changeset's ID:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils undo data.db 1
+
+If any of those rows have been changed again since the changeset the command exits with an error and changes nothing. Use ``--force`` to undo anyway.
+
+.. _cli_history_prune:
+
+Pruning history
+---------------
+
+History grows with every change. The ``prune-history`` command forgets the individual changes to a table up to and including a version (or timestamp), keeping one record for each row that existed at that point. It outputs the number of change records it removed:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils prune-history data.db places 42
+
+After pruning, ``as-of`` and ``restore`` no longer accept versions earlier than that one.
+
+.. note::
+    In Python: :ref:`table.enable_history() <python_api_history>`  CLI reference: :ref:`sqlite-utils enable-history <cli_ref_enable_history>`, :ref:`sqlite-utils history <cli_ref_history>`
+
