@@ -91,6 +91,8 @@ A migration registered with ``transactional=False`` runs without a wrapping tran
 
 Avoid calling ``db.commit()`` or otherwise managing transactions manually inside a transactional migration - register the migration with ``transactional=False`` if it needs to control its own transactions. Using ``with db.atomic():`` blocks inside a migration is fine: they nest as savepoints within the migration's transaction, so the migration as a whole still commits or rolls back as a single unit. See :ref:`python_api_transactions`.
 
+If the database has tables with :ref:`history enabled <python_api_history>`, the transaction of each transactional migration is a :ref:`changeset <python_api_history_changesets>` with the message ``Migration <set>:<name>``. The rows a migration changed in those tables can then be listed with ``db.changes(changeset=...)`` and put back with ``db.undo_changeset()``. A migration applied while a changeset is already open becomes part of that changeset instead.
+
