diff --git a/docs/python-api.rst b/docs/python-api.rst
index d515642..4ca67dc 100644
--- a/docs/python-api.rst
+++ b/docs/python-api.rst
@@ -3091,6 +3091,207 @@ If the ``_counts`` table ever becomes out-of-sync with the actual table counts y
 .. note::
     In the CLI: :ref:`sqlite-utils enable-counts <cli_enable_counts>`
 
+.. _python_api_history:
+
+Tracking changes with history
+=============================
+
+``sqlite-utils`` can keep a record of every change made to the rows of a table. With history enabled you can see who changed what and when, read a table as it was at an earlier point, put rows or whole tables back, and undo a group of changes in one go.
+
+Call ``table.enable_history()`` to start recording:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.table("places").enable_history()
+
+History is recorded by SQLite triggers, so every change is captured however it is made: by the methods in this library, by SQL passed to ``db.execute()``, or by another program writing to the same database file. The rows already in the table are recorded as its starting point.
+
+Everything the feature stores lives in tables with names that start with ``_history_``. These are managed for you - ``db.enable_counts()`` leaves them out, and history cannot be enabled for them.
+
+``table.has_history`` tells you if history is enabled for a table, and ``db.history_tables()`` lists the tables that have it::
+
+    >>> db.table("places").has_history
+    True
+    >>> db.history_tables()
+    ['places']
+
+History cannot be enabled for views, virtual tables or ``WITHOUT ROWID`` tables - these raise ``sqlite_utils.history.HistoryError``, the exception used for every history operation that cannot be carried out.
+
+``table.disable_history()`` stops recording and deletes everything recorded for that table.
+
+.. _python_api_history_versions:
+
+Versions
+--------
+
+Every recorded change - one row inserted, updated or deleted - gets a version number. Versions are shared by the whole database: the first change recorded is version 1 and each later change, in any table, is one higher. Version numbers are never used twice.
+
+``db.history_version`` is the version of the newest change, or ``0`` if nothing has been recorded. ``db.history_version_at(timestamp)`` is the newest version recorded at or before a point in time, given as a ``datetime`` or an ISO 8601 string. Values without a timezone are UTC::
+
+    >>> db.history_version
+    1204
+    >>> db.history_version_at("2026-11-01 09:00")
+    911
+
+These are the rules for what gets recorded:
+
+- An ``UPDATE`` that leaves every recorded column of a row as it was records nothing.
+- Changing a row's primary key (or the ``rowid`` of a table without one) is recorded as a delete of the old row followed by an insert of the new one.
+- Replacing a row, for example with ``insert(..., replace=True)``, is recorded as a delete followed by an insert.
+- Changes that are rolled back are not recorded, and use up no version numbers.
+
+.. _python_api_history_changes:
+
+Reading the recorded changes
+----------------------------
+
+``table.changes()`` iterates over the changes recorded for a table, oldest first. ``db.changes()`` does the same for every table with history enabled. Both accept ``since=`` (only versions higher than this), ``until=`` (only versions up to and including this) and ``changeset=`` (see :ref:`python_api_history_changesets`).
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    last_seen = db.history_version
+    # ... later ...
+    for change in db.changes(since=last_seen):
+        print(change.version, change.table, change.op, change.pk)
+
+``table.history(pk_values)`` returns the list of changes for a single row, which stays available after the row has been deleted.
+
+Each change is a ``sqlite_utils.history.Change`` with these attributes:
+
+``version``
+    The version number of the change.
+
+``table``
+    The name of the table.
+
+``op``
+    ``"insert"``, ``"update"`` or ``"delete"``.
+
+``pk``
+    The primary key of the row - a tuple for compound primary keys, the ``rowid`` for tables without a primary key.
+
+``row``
+    A dictionary of the recorded columns after the change. For a delete, the values the row had just before it was deleted.
+
+``changed``
+    For an update, a dictionary mapping each column that changed to an ``(old, new)`` tuple. Empty for inserts and deletes.
+
+``changeset``
+    The ID of the changeset the change belongs to, or ``None``.
+
+``at``
+    When the change was recorded, as a UTC string such as ``"2026-11-13 09:30:00.123"``.
+
+.. _python_api_history_time_travel:
+
+Time travel
+-----------
+
+``table.as_of(version)`` iterates over the rows of the table as they were when ``version`` was the newest change in the database, in primary key order. ``table.get_as_of(pk_values, version)`` returns a single row, raising ``NotFoundError`` if it did not exist at that version.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    yesterday = db.history_version_at("2026-11-12")
+    for row in db.table("places").as_of(yesterday):
+        print(row)
+
+Rows are returned with the columns the table has now. A column that did not exist yet at that version is ``None``.
+
+``table.diff(from_version, to_version)`` lists the rows that differ between two versions as ``sqlite_utils.history.RowDiff`` objects. Each has a ``pk``, an ``op`` of ``"insert"``, ``"update"`` or ``"delete"``, and ``before`` and ``after`` dictionaries - ``None`` where the row did not exist. ``to_version`` defaults to the current version.
+
+.. _python_api_history_restore:
+
+Restoring rows and tables
+-------------------------
+
+``table.revert(pk_values, version)`` puts one row back to how it was at a version. A row that has been deleted since is created again, and a row that did not exist then is deleted. It returns ``True`` if the table was changed.
+
+``table.restore(version)`` does the same for the whole table and returns the number of rows it inserted, updated or deleted. It makes all of its changes in one transaction.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    places = db.table("places")
+    places.revert(42, version=911)
+    places.restore(911)
+
+Restoring is recorded like any other change, so nothing is lost: a restore can itself be reverted.
+
+.. _python_api_history_changesets:
+
+Changesets
+----------
+
+A changeset groups changes together and gives them a message and an author. Use ``db.changeset()`` as a context manager:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    with db.changeset("Import March data", author="simon") as changeset:
+        db.table("places").insert_all(rows)
+        db.execute("update places set checked = 0")
+    print(changeset.id)
+
+Every change recorded inside the block, in any table and by any means, belongs to the changeset. The block runs in a transaction: if it raises an exception its changes are rolled back and no changeset is kept. A changeset that recorded no changes is discarded. Changesets cannot be nested.
+
+``db.changesets()`` returns a list of ``sqlite_utils.history.Changeset`` objects with ``id``, ``message``, ``author`` and ``created_at`` attributes.
+
+``db.undo_changeset(changeset_id)`` puts every row the changeset changed back to how it was just before the changeset. The undo is recorded as a new changeset with the message ``Undo changeset <id>``, which is returned - so an undo can be undone too.
+
+If any of those rows has been changed again since the changeset, ``sqlite_utils.history.HistoryConflict`` is raised and nothing is changed. Its ``conflicts`` attribute is a list of ``(table_name, pk)`` tuples. Pass ``force=True`` to undo anyway, overwriting the later changes.
+
+:ref:`Migrations <migrations>` that run in a transaction are recorded as a changeset named ``Migration <set>:<name>`` when the database has tables with history enabled.
+
+.. _python_api_history_schema:
+
+History and schema changes
+--------------------------
+
+History keeps working when a table is changed using this library:
+
+- ``table.add_column()`` starts recording the new column.
+- ``table.transform()`` keeps the history. Renamed columns are renamed in the recorded history as well, dropped columns are removed from it.
+- ``db.rename_table()`` takes the history along to the new name.
+- ``table.drop()`` deletes the history together with the table.
+
+After any of these, ``table.as_of(db.history_version)`` still matches the table. Where a schema change alters stored values - a new column with a default, or a type change that converts values - the new values are recorded as updates.
+
+If you change a table in some other way, for example with ``ALTER TABLE``, call ``table.enable_history()`` again. This brings the tracking up to date with the table's columns and records anything that differs from the last recorded state, keeping the existing history.
+
+.. _python_api_history_exclude:
+
+Excluding columns
+-----------------
+
+Use ``exclude=`` to leave columns out of the history, for example a counter that changes all the time:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.table("pages").enable_history(exclude=["hits"])
+
+Changes that only touch excluded columns are not recorded, and restoring leaves those columns alone. ``table.history_columns`` lists the columns that are recorded. Primary key columns cannot be excluded.
+
+Call ``enable_history(exclude=[...])`` again to change the excluded columns.
+
+.. _python_api_history_prune:
+
+Pruning history
+---------------
+
+``table.prune_history(version)`` forgets the individual changes up to and including a version, to save space. Each row that existed at that version keeps a single record as its starting point. It returns the number of change records removed.
+
+After pruning, the table can still be read or restored as of that version or any later one. Earlier versions raise ``HistoryError``. ``table.history_floor`` is the oldest version that can still be used, ``0`` for a table that has never been pruned.
+
+.. _python_api_history_legacy:
+
+Importing history from sqlite-history
+-------------------------------------
+
+Tables that were tracked by the older `sqlite-history <https://github.com/simonw/sqlite-history>`__ plugin have a ``_<table>_history`` table and three triggers. The first time ``enable_history()`` is called for such a table those records are imported, keeping their timestamps, and the old table and triggers are removed.
+
+Pass ``import_legacy=False`` to leave them untouched and start a fresh history instead.
+
+.. note::
+    In the CLI: :ref:`sqlite-utils enable-history <cli_history>`
+
